---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GameCTA from "../components/GameCTA.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Start here"
  description="For the moment your brain goes blank under pressure: why it happens, what to say first, and where to go next."
  canonical="/start-here/"
  image="/assets/pages/start-here-hero.webp"
  imageAlt="A pressure moment paused long enough to choose the next line."
>
  <article class="article-shell">
    <header class="content-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Start here</p>
        <h1>When your brain goes blank, start smaller.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          You know the feeling. The conversation ends, your body finally unclenches, and the perfect
          sentence strolls in three hours late like it pays rent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img src="/assets/pages/start-here-hero.webp" alt="" width="1600" height="900" />
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <p>
        In the actual moment, though, you froze. Or you said yes too fast. Or you started explaining,
        then explaining the explanation, then somehow apologizing for needing oxygen and a calendar.
      </p>
      <p>
        That does not mean you are weak. It means pressure got faster than your words. Your system
        reached for the oldest exits: freeze, fawn, over-explain, agree, smooth it over, survive the
        next ten seconds.
      </p>

      <h2>Freezing is not a character flaw</h2>
      <p>
        A blank mind under pressure is normal. Annoying, yes. Deeply inconvenient at meetings and
        dinner tables, also yes. But not a moral defect. Your body is trying to keep the situation from
        getting worse, and sometimes it picks the fastest-looking door.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is that the fastest door is often expensive. The instant yes becomes a weekend you
        did not have. The apology becomes permission for the original issue to vanish. The paragraph of
        reasons becomes a nice buffet for someone who wants handles.
      </p>

      <h2>You do not need sudden courage</h2>
      <p>
        Courage is great. Also unreliable. Prepared words are better. You need a few lines you can reach
        while your brain is still buffering, and you need to spot the move quickly enough to avoid
        entering the wrong conversation.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole job of this site: name the pressure, pick the move, borrow a script. No grand
        transformation arc required. We are trying to get you one usable sentence sooner.
      </p>

      <h2>The three doors</h2>
      <p>
        If you want to understand what is happening, start with <a href="/patterns/">Patterns</a>. These
        are the pressure moves: guilt trip, gaslighting, moving goalposts, public shaming, and the rest
        of the little machines that make you defend yourself before you decide.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you know the pattern but need a move back, use <a href="/techniques/">Techniques</a>. Grey
        rock, broken record, boundary, labeling: short tools for staying boringly steady when the room
        wants a speech.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you are in a specific scene and need words now, go to <a href="/situations/">Situations</a>.
        Those pages are the worked conversations: boss expanding the ask, parent guilt trips, public
        jokes, silent treatment, the tone trial.
      </p>

      <h2>Five lines for any pressured moment</h2>
      <p>
        If you read nothing else, take these. They are not magic. They are a pause with shoes on.
      </p>
      <div class="lead-script-list">
        <figure class="script-block">
          <figcaption>Buy one second</figcaption>
          <blockquote><p>"I need a second to answer that clearly."</p></blockquote>
        </figure>
        <figure class="script-block">
          <figcaption>Do not commit yet</figcaption>
          <blockquote><p>"I need to check before I commit."</p></blockquote>
        </figure>
        <figure class="script-block">
          <figcaption>Slow the room down</figcaption>
          <blockquote><p>"I am not answering this on the spot."</p></blockquote>
        </figure>
        <figure class="script-block">
          <figcaption>Reset a rushed yes</figcaption>
          <blockquote><p>"I answered too quickly. I need to revisit that."</p></blockquote>
        </figure>
        <figure class="script-block">
          <figcaption>Make pressure choose</figcaption>
          <blockquote><p>"If you need an answer right now, it is no for now."</p></blockquote>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <p>
        Start there. Not with the perfect speech. Not with proving you are reasonable enough to have a
        boundary. One pause. One sentence. Then the next one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <GameCTA
      type="situation"
      hook="Rehearse the moment pressure tries to choose your answer for you."
    />
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
